    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>Left ankle pain and swelling after tripping on a curb this morning. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Gehrig called the clinic and was seen the same day. This morning, on a short walk to buy a newspaper, he caught the toe of his left shoe on a curb, rolled the ankle inward and fell onto his hands. He was not wearing his ankle-foot orthosis, which arrived last week, because he was only going a block. He got up by himself and walked home with a limp. The outside of the ankle has swollen and aches, 5 of 10. He did not hit his head, has no wrist or hand pain, and did not black out or feel faint beforehand. He takes riluzole twice daily and is due for his two-month liver test. He has no breathlessness or trouble swallowing. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>This morning, tripping on a curb with the left foot</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Outside of the left ankle</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>Inversion; foot drop; orthosis not worn</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>5 of 10; can walk with a limp</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No head injury, faintness, or wrist pain</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>ALS with left ankle weakness; riluzole for two months</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

